    "monograph": "## **ERTAPENEM (Invanz) - Complete Monograph**\n\n\n### **Category**\n\n\nCarbapenem (Group 2 carbapenem - lacks antipseudomonal activity)\n\n\n### **Mechanism**\n\n\nBinds to penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), primarily PBP-2 and PBP-3, inhibiting bacterial cell wall synthesis. Stable against hydrolysis by penicillinases, cephalosporinases, and extended-spectrum beta-lactamases (ESBLs). Hydrolyzed by metallo-beta-lactamases (MBLs). Exhibits time-dependent bactericidal activity (T>MIC optimization).\n\n\n### **Indications**\n\n- Complicated intra-abdominal infections (cIAI)\n- Community-acquired pneumonia (CAP)\n- Complicated urinary tract infections (cUTI) including pyelonephritis\n- Complicated skin and skin structure infections (cSSSI)\n- Acute pelvic infections (gynecologic infections, postpartum endomyometritis, septic abortion)\n- Diabetic foot infections (without osteomyelitis)\n- Surgical prophylaxis for colorectal surgery\n\n### **Coverage**\n\n\n**Gram-positive (aerobic):**\n\n- _Staphylococcus aureus_ (MSSA only)\n- _Streptococcus pneumoniae_ (penicillin-susceptible)\n- _Streptococcus pyogenes_\n- _Streptococcus agalactiae_\n\n**Gram-negative (aerobic):**\n\n- Enterobacteriaceae: _E. coli_, _Klebsiella_ spp., _Proteus mirabilis_, _P. vulgaris_, _Citrobacter_ spp., _Enterobacter_ spp., _Serratia marcescens_, _Morganella morganii_, _Providencia_ spp.\n- _Haemophilus influenzae_, _Moraxella catarrhalis_\n- ESBL-producing Enterobacteriaceae\n\n**Anaerobes:**\n\n- _Bacteroides fragilis_ and _Bacteroides_ spp.\n- _Clostridium perfringens_ (not _C. difficile_)\n- _Fusobacterium_ spp.\n- _Peptostreptococcus_ spp.\n- _Prevotella_ spp.\n\n**NO ACTIVITY against:**\n\n- ❌ _Pseudomonas aeruginosa_\n- ❌ _Acinetobacter_ spp.\n- ❌ MRSA / methicillin-resistant staphylococci\n- ❌ _Enterococcus_ spp. (including VRE)\n- ❌ Carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae (CRE)\n- ❌ _Stenotrophomonas maltophilia_\n\n### **Adult Dose**\n\n\n**Standard:** 1 g IV/IM once daily\n\n- Duration: 3–14 days depending on indication\n- Diabetic foot infections: up to 4 weeks\n- Surgical prophylaxis: 1 g IV given 1 hour before incision\n\n### **Renal Dose (HD, CRRT)**\n\n\n| CrCl       | Dose                                                                             |\n| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| >30 mL/min | 1 g daily (no adjustment)                                                        |\n| ≤30 mL/min | 500 mg daily                                                                     |\n| **HD**     | 500 mg daily; if dose given <6 hours before HD, give supplemental 150 mg post-HD |\n| **CRRT**   | Limited data; 500 mg–1 g daily suggested based on clearance                      |\n| **PD**     | No data available                                                                |\n\n\n### **Hepatic Dose**\n\n\nNo adjustment required (hepatic metabolism is negligible)\n\n\n### **Pediatric Dose**\n\n- **3 months to 12 years:** 15 mg/kg IV/IM every 12 hours (max 1 g/day)\n- **>12 years:** 1 g IV/IM once daily (adult dosing)\n- **<3 months:** Not recommended (no data)\n- **Not recommended for meningitis** (inadequate CNS penetration)\n\n### **Side Effects**\n\n\n**Common (≥1%):**\n\n- Diarrhea (5%), nausea (3%), vomiting\n- Headache\n- Infusion site reactions (phlebitis, pain)\n- Elevated ALT/AST\n\n**Serious/CNS toxicity:**\n\n- Seizures\n- Encephalopathy (confusion, altered mental status)\n- Hallucinations (often visual)\n- Myoclonus, tremor\n- _C. difficile_associated diarrhea (CDAD)\n- Hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis\n\n### **Monitor**\n\n- **Renal function:** SCr, CrCl (baseline and during therapy)\n- **Neurological status:** Mental status changes, seizure activity, hallucinations – especially in high-risk patients\n- **Serum albumin:** Hypoalbuminemia increases free drug and CNS toxicity risk\n- **Signs of hypersensitivity:** Rash, anaphylaxis\n- **LFTs:** Periodic monitoring\n- **Signs of superinfection:** _C. difficile_, candidiasis\n\n### **Drug Interactions**\n\n\n| Drug                                      | Interaction                                                  | Management                                               |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Valproic acid**                         | ↓ VPA levels by 60–95% within 24 hrs → breakthrough seizures | **AVOID** – use alternative antibiotic or anticonvulsant |\n| **Probenecid**                            | ↓ Renal excretion of ertapenem, ↑ half-life                  | Coadministration not recommended                         |\n| **Live vaccines** (BCG, typhoid, cholera) | ↓ Vaccine efficacy                                           | Avoid concurrent use                                     |\n| **Oral contraceptives**                   | May ↓ estrogen levels via altered gut flora                  | Low risk; consider backup contraception                  |\n\n\n### **Pregnancy**\n\n- **Former FDA Category B** (now risk summary system)\n- Animal studies: No evidence of teratogenicity at exposures up to 1.2× human dose (rats) and 3× human dose (mice)\n- Insufficient human data to inform drug-associated risks for birth defects\n- Use only if benefit outweighs risk\n\n### **Breastfeeding**\n\n- Present in breast milk at low levels (<0.13–0.38 mcg/mL)\n- Not expected to cause adverse effects in breastfed infants; acceptable in nursing mothers\n- Monitor infant for GI disturbances (diarrhea, thrush)\n\n### **Notes**\n\n\n**CNS Toxicity Risk Factors:**\n\n- Advanced age (median 71.5 years in case series)\n- Renal dysfunction (half-life extends from 4.4 hrs → 14–19 hrs in ESRD)\n- Hypoalbuminemia (86% of neurotoxicity cases had albumin <3.5 g/dL)\n- Pre-existing CNS disease (stroke, seizure history, brain lesions)\n- Symptom onset: median 4–5 days after initiation\n\n**Clinical Pearls:**\n\n- Once-daily dosing due to long half-life (~4 hours) and high protein binding (~95%)\n- Lower seizure potential than imipenem (acidic C-2 side chain → less GABA antagonism)\n- Consider meropenem or alternative if _Pseudomonas_ or _Acinetobacter_ suspected\n- Previous stroke should be considered a relative contraindication\n- Most patients (90.9%) recover completely after drug discontinuation; median recovery 7 days\n- Contains ~137 mg (6 mEq) sodium per 1 g vial\n- IM injection prepared with lidocaine (contraindicated if lidocaine allergy)\n\n---\n\n\n**Corrections needed in your current entry:**\n\n1. **Remove MRSA from Coverage** – ertapenem has NO activity against MRSA\n2. Add Enterobacteriaceae, anaerobes to coverage (color-coded appropriately)\n3. Complete all empty fields as above\n\nWould you like me to format this for direct Notion database integration?",
