    "monograph": "[teicoplanin 仿單](https://www.cth.org.tw/public/medi_news/57c69513d4a9ef5843abb5e3dd351acf.pdf)\n\n\n## What to monitor and be aware of when using teicoplanin\n\n## 1. Therapeutic Drug Monitoring (TDM) of Teicoplanin\n\n\n### Target Trough Concentrations\n\n\n| Infection Severity            | Target Trough (Cmin) |\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------- |\n| Mild/uncomplicated infections | **10-15 mg/L**       |\n| Deep-seated/severe infections | **15-30 mg/L**       |\n| Endocarditis/bone infections  | **20-30 mg/L**       |\n\n\n### TDM Timing\n\n- Timing for measuring TDM was mostly 72–96 hours or longer after the initiation of the first dose, which was expected to be a steady-state.\n- First trough level should be drawn on **Day 3-5** (after loading doses complete)\n- Because of the extremely long half-life, a loading dose for 2 to 3 days is essential during teicoplanin therapy to achieve an early optimal concentration.\n\n### Loading Dose Importance\n\n- A loading dose of 10–12 mg/kg or 600–800 mg is recommended to achieve trough levels of ≥15 or ≥20 mg/L.\n- The proportion of trough levels achieving ≥10 mg/L is only 20% (days 3–5) or 38% (days 6–8) in the standard dose group without adequate loading.\n\n---\n\n\n## 2. Staphylococcus capitis – Clinical Concerns\n\n\n### NRCS-A Clone (Multidrug-Resistant)\n\n\nThis is a particularly important concern, especially in NICU settings:\n\n- NRCS-A harbours a multidrug resistance profile (including resistance to the usual first-line antibiotics used in NICUs). It is also able to adapt under vancomycin selective pressure.\n- The NRCS-A clone exhibited reduced susceptibility to teicoplanin, daptomycin, and gentamicin.\n- Acquired vancomycin resistance was associated with a stable cross-resistance to teicoplanin (final MICs between 12 and 48 mg/L).\n\n### Resistance Pattern Monitoring\n\n\n**Verify susceptibility testing** – Key points:\n\n- Check MIC values for teicoplanin (EUCAST breakpoint: ≤2 mg/L susceptible)\n- Be aware of heteroresistance to glycopeptides\n- Pulsotype NRCS-A isolates constantly showed either vancomycin resistance (37.5%) or heteroresistance (62.5%).\n\n---\n\n\n## 3. Adverse Effects & Safety Monitoring\n\n\n### Laboratory Monitoring Requirements\n\n\n| Parameter                      | Frequency                             | Threshold of Concern                                                                        |\n| ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Renal function (Cr, eGFR)**  | Baseline, then weekly                 | Nephrotoxicity was reported at trough concentrations of ≥60 mg/L.                           |\n| **Hepatic function (LFTs)**    | Baseline, then weekly                 | Transient elevations common                                                                 |\n| **Complete blood count (CBC)** | Baseline, then weekly                 | Thrombocytopenia was observed at trough concentrations of ≥40 mg/L.                         |\n| **Auditory monitoring**        | If prolonged therapy/renal impairment | Ototoxicity (deafness and tinnitus) has been reported in patients treated with teicoplanin. |\n\n\n### Key Adverse Effects\n\n\nIn a series of 3377 patients given teicoplanin, the most common reported adverse events were hypersensitivity (2.6%), abnormal liver function (1.7%), fever (0.8%), local intolerance (1.6%), abnormal renal function (0.7%) and ototoxicity (0.3%).\n\n\n### Drug Interactions to Monitor\n\n- Patients receiving teicoplanin in conjunction with, or sequentially with, other medicinal products with known nephrotoxic/ototoxic potential (aminoglycosides, ciclosporin, cisplatin, furosemide and ethacrynic acid) should be carefully monitored.\n\n---\n\n\n## 4. Catheter Management Considerations\n\n\n### CVC Removal vs. Salvage\n\n- Central venous catheter (CVC) removal versus in situ treatment with CoNS CLABSIs is a controversial treatment strategy with no clear consensus.\n- Among the 25 patients, 17 CVCs were removed after diagnosis, leading to a 76.5% eradication rate in this group.\n- If tunneled CVC- or ID-related bacteremia is uncomplicated and the CVC or port is not removed, infections due to coagulase-negative staphylococci should be treated with systemic and antimicrobial lock therapy for 14 days.\n\n### Treatment Duration\n\n- **Uncomplicated CoNS CRBSI with catheter removal:** 5-7 days\n- **Catheter retention:** 10-14 days with antibiotic lock therapy\n- A short course of antibiotic treatment (≤3 days) is safe after catheter withdrawal in catheter-related bloodstream infections due to coagulase-negative staphylococci.\n\n---\n\n\n## 5. Clinical Response Monitoring\n\n\n### Indicators of Treatment Success\n\n- Defervescence within 48-72 hours\n- Repeat blood cultures negative at 48-72 hours\n- Resolution of signs/symptoms of infection\n- Trough concentrations within therapeutic range\n\n### Warning Signs of Treatment Failure\n\n- Persistent bacteremia >72 hours\n- Clinical deterioration\n- New metastatic foci of infection\n- Rising inflammatory markers despite therapy\n- Consider NRCS-A clone if teicoplanin/vancomycin failure occurs\n\n---\n\n\n## Summary Checklist\n\n\n**Before initiating teicoplanin:**\n\n- [ ] Confirm organism susceptibility and MIC\n- [ ] Baseline renal/hepatic function and CBC\n- [ ] Assess for drug interactions\n\n**During therapy:**\n\n- [ ] Adequate loading dose administered\n- [ ] Trough level on Day 3-5 (target ≥10-15 mg/L)\n- [ ] Weekly renal function, LFTs, CBC\n- [ ] Repeat blood cultures at 48-72 hours\n- [ ] Clinical reassessment at 72 hours\n- [ ] Consider CVC removal if poor response\n\n**Special alerts:**\n\n- [ ] Be vigilant for NRCS-A clone (especially NICU settings)\n- [ ] Monitor for cross-resistance if treatment failure\n- [ ] Consider linezolid or daptomycin as alternatives if glycopeptide resistance suspected",
